\begin{abstract}
The shift from fifth-generation (5G) to sixth generation (6G) wireless networks places strict demands on antennas: extreme data rates, sub-millisecond latency, massive device density, and centimetre-level sensing accuracy that classical antennas cannot satisfy. This paper reviews antenna architectures proposed for beyond-5G and 6G systems, including massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) , millimetre-wave (mmWave) phased arrays, sub terahertz (sub THz) and terahertz (THz) antennas, frequency, polarization, and pattern reconfigurable antennas, dielectric resonator antennas (DRAs), antenna-in-package (AiP) modules, reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RIS), dynamic metasurface and holographic MIMO antennas, and fluid antenna systems (FAS). For each technology, the governing electromagnetic principles are summarized alongside quantitative performance figures, gain, bandwidth, element count, and switching or reconfiguration speed, drawn from 15 recently published peer reviewed antenna designs and compared directly in a dedicated table. The paper also analyzes open engineering bottlenecks, including propagation loss, mutual coupling, phase quantization error, thermal budget, fabrication tolerance, and control channel overhead, and outlines research directions toward AI assisted, highly integrated, and dynamically reconfigurable 6G antenna platforms.


\end{abstract}

