\section{Introduction}
Wireless communication has progressed significantly from early cellular systems to 5G, which offers enhanced mobile broadband, massive machine type communication, and ultra reliable low latency communication. However, future applications like extended reality and autonomous systems are expected to require capabilities beyond current 5G networks. \cite{ref1,ref2,ref3}.


6G is being explored as the next major step in cellular technology, with goals like 1 Tb/s peak data rates, sub millisecond latency, and support for over $10^7$ devices/km². Hitting those targets will require major advances in network design, signal processing, RF circuits, and especially antenna technology, which is the physical link between the transceiver and the radio channel.\cite{ref35,ref36,ref37}. 

Antennas determine important characteristics of a wireless system including gain $G$, radiation efficiency $\eta$, half power beamwidth, polarization purity, spectral efficiency, and the number of independent spatial streams available for multiplexing. The Friis transmission equation,
\begin{equation}
\frac{P_r}{P_t} = G_t G_r \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d}\right)^2,
\label{eq:friis}
\end{equation}
shows directly why the transition toward millimetre-wave (mmWave, 24--100~GHz) and sub terahertz (sub THz, 100-300~GHz) frequencies is double edged: the much shorter wavelength $\lambda$ permits dense packing of high gain array elements within a compact aperture, but the same equation shows received power falling as $\lambda^2$, so free space path loss increases by roughly 20~dB per decade of frequency before atmospheric absorption and blockage are even considered \cite{ref6,ref7}.

More recently, reconfigurable antennas, reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RIS), dynamic metasurface antennas, holographic MIMO surfaces, and fluid antenna systems (FAS) have drawn significant attention as potential physical layer enablers for 6G. \cite{ref41,ref42,ref43}.

