\section{Antenna Requirements for 6G}
Future 6G systems will require highly versatile antenna solutions that simultaneously operate across broad ranges of frequencies, bandwidths, beam directions, and form factors. Key antenna requirements include high gain to close the mmWave/THz link budget, wide instantaneous bandwidth for multi gigabit throughput, a low profile design for integration into handsets and vehicles, high radiation efficiency to preserve battery and RF front end power, electronic beam steering and reconfigurability for dynamic environments, and dense spatial multiplexing to scale capacity \cite{ref15,ref17}.

Millimetre wave and sub terahertz frequencies provide much larger absolute bandwidths than traditional sub 6 GHz cellular bands. For instance, the availability of both unlicensed and licensed spectrum at 60 GHz and 140 GHz provides hundreds of MHz of tightly spaced spectrums while, in contrast, below 6 GHz only tens of MHz are available on average. However, these higher frequencies also bring proportionally greater free space path loss, oxygen/water vapour absorption, and non line of sight blockage. As a result, high gain arrays with adaptive, low latency beamforming are not optional refinements but essential system components at these bands. Table~\ref{tab:requirements} summarizes the resulting antenna requirements.

