\section{Massive MIMO Antennas}
Massive MIMO employs a large number $N$ of antenna elements at the base station to serve multiple users simultaneously over the same time-frequency resource. As $N$ grows, channel vectors to spatially separated users become asymptotically orthogonal, so that with matched-filter or zero-forcing precoding, inter-user interference and small-scale fading effects average out --- the theoretical basis first established by Marzetta \cite{ref8} and extended in \cite{ref9,ref10,ref11}.

In practice, scaling $N$ introduces mutual coupling between closely spaced elements, RF chain cost and DC power that scale linearly with $N$, phase to amplitude calibration error across the array, and increased physical footprint. Hybrid analog digital beamforming addresses the RF chain problem by connecting a small number $N_{RF} \ll N$ of RF chains to the full array through an analog phase shifting network, factorizing the digital precoder $\mathbf{F}_{BB}$ and the analog precoder $\mathbf{F}_{RF}$ so that the effective precoder $\mathbf{F} = \mathbf{F}_{RF}\mathbf{F}_{BB}$ approximates the fully digital optimum at a fraction of the hardware cost \cite{ref12,ref13}.

