\section{Millimetre-Wave Antennas}
Millimetre wave communication (24-100~GHz) is a cornerstone technology for 5G and future 6G systems because it provides access to substantially wider bandwidth than conventional cellular bands \cite{ref6}.
Beamforming is essential at mmWave because free space path loss at 28~GHz is roughly 20~dB higher than at 2.4~GHz over the same distance, and mmWave links are additionally sensitive to human body and foliage blockage. Hybrid beamforming, combining a modest number of RF chains with a large analog phased array, is the dominant architecture for reducing power consumption and ADC/DAC cost while retaining most of the achievable beamforming gain \cite{ref13,ref14}.

Representative implementations quantify these trade offs. A 4 element mmWave MIMO array on 0.254~mm Rogers RT5880 substrate operating at 37~GHz raises the single-element gain of 6.84~dB to 12.8~dB through array combining while maintaining envelope correlation coefficient (ECC) within the 5G MIMO diversity threshold and radiation efficiency above 85\% \cite{ref46}. Scaling the same design philosophy to a 64 element base station array yields 21.2~dBi gain at 37.2~GHz, while a $4\times8$ wideband array reported for 5G n78 base stations achieves a maximum realized gain near 22~dBi with 8 independently steerable beams in azimuth \cite{ref45}.

