\section{Sub-THz and Terahertz Antennas}
Sub-terahertz (100--300~GHz) and terahertz (0.3--10~THz) communication are promising technologies for achieving multi tens of Gb/s to Tb/s data rates in 6G. \cite{ref15,ref16,ref38,ref39,ref40}. At these frequencies molecular absorption principally by atmospheric oxygen and water vapour creates narrow low loss transmission windows separated by high attenuation bands, so THz link budgets must be planned around these windows rather than treated as uniformly available spectrum \cite{ref17,ref18}.

Because wavelength shrinks to below 1~mm, THz antennas can integrate very large element counts in a chip scale footprint, but conductor loss, surface roughness, and dielectric loss tangent all increase relative to their electrical size, so radiation efficiency becomes the dominant design constraint rather than mutual coupling. Two complementary design philosophies dominate the literature: quasi optical resonant cavity antennas that maximize gain through constructive multiple reflection, and planar array antennas (microstrip, substrate integrated waveguide (SIW), or grounded coplanar waveguide (GCPW) fed) that maximize integration density.

A fully metallic Fabry Perot cavity (FPC) antenna operating at 300~GHz, built from seven laser cut brass layers with an aperture frequency selective surface (FSS) partially reflective layer, achieves a measured 17.7~dBi peak gain at 289~GHz with gain above 14.4~dBi sustained from 285--310~GHz and a 22~GHz (282--304~GHz) impedance bandwidth, in a compact $2.6\,\text{mm}\times2.6\,\text{mm}$ footprint --- demonstrating that quasi optical cavity structures can deliver base station class gain at sub THz frequencies without active elements \cite{ref47}. A complementary GCPW fed, SIW backed three layer planar array operating at 0.6366~THz achieves a 13.14~dBi peak gain with a 0.61-0.68~THz ($\approx$70~GHz) impedance bandwidth using a metal fence structure to suppress inter patch coupling, illustrating the planar array route to true THz-band (rather than sub THz) operation \cite{ref48}.

