\section{Reconfigurable Antennas}
Reconfigurable antennas can dynamically modify frequency, radiation pattern, or polarization by electronically altering the current distribution on the radiating structure, typically using PIN diodes, varactor diodes, RF MEMS switches, or liquid-crystal/liquid-metal tuning elements connected across gaps or stubs in the patch or slot geometry. Switching a PIN diode between forward-biased (low impedance, ON) and reverse biased (high impedance, OFF) states effectively lengthens or shortens the resonant current path, shifting the resonant frequency $f_r \propto 1/L_{eff}$ according to the modified effective electrical length $L_{eff}$ of the radiator \cite{ref30,ref31}.

A five mode, nine band frequency reconfigurable monopole using four PIN diodes on FR 4 substrate covers single bands at 3.5/5~GHz, dual bands at 2.1/5.6~GHz and 2.6/6.5~GHz, and a triple band at 1.8/4.8/6.4~GHz, with VSWR below 1.5 across all modes, 84\% radiation efficiency, and gain from 1.2-3.6~dBi, confirming that PIN diode switching networks can realistically reconfigure across nine distinct operating bands from a single compact aperture \cite{ref59}. At millimetre wave frequencies, an 8-mode, three PIN diode patch antenna switches between mid 5G bands and high 5G bands from a single $20\,\text{mm}\times25\,\text{mm}$ FR 4 aperture, with radiation efficiency of 75-98\% across modes demonstrating that a single reconfigurable radiator can bridge sub 6~GHz and mmWave 5G/6G operation, eliminating the need for separate antenna subsystems per band.

Switching losses in the diode's ON state series resistance, DC bias network radiation leakage that distorts the pattern, non linear diode behaviour under high RF power, and increased fabrication complexity from the added switching and biasing circuitry remain the principal engineering costs of reconfigurability, and typically explain why reconfigurable antenna gain (a few dBi) trails purpose built fixed frequency array gain (10+~dBi) at a given frequency.

\section{Dielectric Resonator Antennas}
Dielectric resonator antennas (DRAs) radiate from resonant modes excited within a low loss, high permittivity ceramic or polymer volume rather than from surface current on a metal patch, so unlike microstrip antennas they exhibit no conductor loss and typically achieve radiation efficiency exceeding 90\% even at millimetre wave frequencies where metallic loss becomes severe. DRAs support multiple resonant modes (e.g., TE, TM, and hybrid HEM modes) simultaneously, giving inherent flexibility for wideband or multiband operation and for MIMO/array configurations without additional radiating structures \cite{ref28,ref29}.

A DRA exciting the higher order TE$_{\delta 15x}$ mode with a hollow central cylinder to lower the quality factor $Q$ achieves 10.7\% and 16.1\% impedance bandwidth at 14.3-15.9~GHz and 14.1-16.5~GHz respectively, showing that mode selection and $Q$ spoiling geometry are the primary bandwidth control levers in DRA design \cite{ref51}. Dual band operation spanning both sub 6~GHz and mmWave 5G bands from a single composite DRA an annular low permittivity radiator concentrically integrated with a high permittivity cylindrical DRA achieves a large frequency ratio between the two resonances from a single co planar waveguide feed, illustrating a route to multiband coverage without duplicating hardware \cite{ref52}. Bandwidth extension techniques such as metal ring loading and cavity backed slot feeding have independently pushed measured DRA bandwidth beyond 25\% while lifting peak gain from a baseline of roughly 5~dBi to 7+~dBi, closing part of the gain gap relative to metallic patch arrays. The principal practical drawback remains precision dielectric fabrication achieving tight tolerance on both permittivity and mechanical dimension simultaneously and the mechanical/thermal integration of a bulk ceramic resonator with planar RF circuitry.


\section{Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces}
A reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) is a planar array of typically hundreds to thousands of passive, sub wavelength unit cells, each capable of imposing an independently controllable phase shift $\varphi_n$ on an incident electromagnetic wave, most commonly through a PIN diode, varactor diode, or liquid crystal element embedded in the unit cell resonator \cite{ref19,ref20,ref41}. By configuring the phase profile so that reflections from all $N$ elements combine coherently at a target receiver, the composite channel gain can, in the idealized far field case, scale as $N^2$ rather than $N$ the theoretical basis for RIS aided coverage extension and virtual LoS creation in obstructed environments \cite{ref21,ref43}.

