Because a fully continuous analog phase response is costly to implement, most fabricated RIS prototypes quantize the phase to 1 bit ($0^{\circ}$/$180^{\circ}$) or a small number of bits; a comprehensive survey of experimentally reported RIS hardware compiles the following representative operating points: a $20\times20$ element 1-bit PIN diode RIS at 28~GHz achieving 27.9~dBi surface gain over 2~GHz bandwidth; a $20\times20$-element 1 bit PIN-diode RIS at 27.5~GHz achieving 24.7~dB gain over a much wider 7~GHz bandwidth; a $24\times24$-element 1 bit RIS at 10.55~GHz achieving 23.3~dBi gain over 2.4~GHz; and a $20\times20$-element analog phase, liquid crystal tuned RIS at 28~GHz achieving 17.9~dBi gain together spanning sub-6~GHz to mmWave operation with gains from roughly 15-28~dBi depending on element count and phase resolution \cite{ref20}. A separate field deployable prototype using 160 elements at 5.8~GHz was integrated into a live wireless link and demonstrated measurable coverage extension gains under realistic multipath conditions, while a more recent 3 bit resolution RIS at 3.6~GHz using a low cost PCB spring contact feed shows that higher phase resolution (reducing quantization error relative to 1 bit designs) is becoming practical at sub 6~GHz without a large manufacturing cost penalty \cite{ref49,ref50}.

Field trials to date report on the order of 10~dB received-signal-strength improvement from a well optimized RIS deployment in coverage extension scenarios \cite{ref22}. 

