\section{Metasurface and Holographic MIMO Antennas}
Metasurfaces consist of two dimensional arrays of engineered sub wavelength scattering elements that impose a spatially varying amplitude and phase response on an incident wave, in effect implementing a generalized form of Snell's law at the surface. Dynamic metasurface antennas (DMAs) exploit this by embedding tunable elements (e.g., varactor loaded slots) along a waveguide feed, so that a single feed can synthesize a large number of controllable radiation patterns without one RF chain per element proposed as a low power alternative to conventional phased arrays for large scale 6G MIMO \cite{ref23,ref42}.

Holographic MIMO surfaces generalize this concept toward an electrically large, quasi continuous radiating aperture conceptually an antenna array with element spacing approaching the sub wavelength (holographic) limit rather than the conventional $\lambda/2$ spacing providing a much larger number of controllable spatial degrees of freedom per unit aperture area than a discrete array of the same physical size, which is the basis of the claimed near field beamfocusing and super directivity gains attributed to holographic MIMO in 6G oriented studies \cite{ref24}. 

