\section{Fluid Antenna Systems}
A fluid antenna system (FAS) replaces the conventional approach of adding more fixed radiating elements with a single (or few) radiating element(s) whose effective position can be switched among $N$ predefined ports distributed over a physical aperture of length $W\lambda$, where $\lambda$ is the carrier wavelength. Denoting the channel gain at the $n$-th port as $h_n$, the spatial correlation between ports $m$ and $n$ under isotropic scattering follows a Bessel function model,
\begin{equation}
\rho_{m,n} = J_0\!\left(\frac{2\pi |m-n| W}{N-1}\right),
\label{eq:fas}
\end{equation}
so ports separated by less than roughly $\lambda/2$ remain correlated while more widely spaced ports fade nearly independently giving FAS access to spatial diversity from a device volume far smaller than an equivalent fixed multi antenna array \cite{ref32,ref33,ref58}.

Early FAS concepts used liquid metal or micro electromechanical actuation to physically relocate a radiating element, which is mechanically slow; more recent hardware realizes port switching electronically. A pixel based reconfigurable antenna applied to FAS demonstrates high electronic switching speed between ports without any mechanical movement, directly addressing the port switching delay bottleneck that otherwise degrades achievable throughput in high reliability low latency (HRLLC) 6G scenarios \cite{ref55}. At millimetre wave frequencies, a surface wave enabled FAS has been designed and experimentally validated for fluid antenna multiple access, extending the FAS concept beyond sub-6~GHz proof of concept demonstrations into the mmWave regime relevant to 6G access links \cite{ref56}. System level analyses show that even a modest number of ports ($N$ on the order of 10-20) distributed over a $1$-$2\lambda$ aperture can approach the outage performance of a much larger conventional selection combining array, at the cost of the CSI acquisition overhead needed to identify the best port before each transmission \cite{ref57}.

Practical FAS implementation must jointly manage port switching delay (which competes directly against the ultra low latency goals FAS is meant to serve), the channel estimation overhead of acquiring CSI at every candidate port, radiation pattern and gain consistency across ports, and long term reliability of the switching mechanism all of which remain active research problems rather than solved engineering issues.

\section{Antenna-in-Package Technology}
Antenna in package (AiP) technology co integrates the radiating elements with RFIC dies and interconnects within a single multilayer package substrate (typically organic build up or low temperature co fired ceramic, LTCC), eliminating the long, lossy transmission lines that would otherwise be needed to route RF signals from a separately packaged IC to an off package antenna board. This is particularly consequential above 24~GHz, where a few millimetres of PCB trace can introduce insertion loss exceeding 1~dB/mm \cite{ref25,ref26,ref27}.
 
A widely cited 28~GHz organic AiP module integrates 64 dual polarized antenna elements with four SiGe BiCMOS phased array ICs via flip chip assembly; direct antenna only measurements show over 3~GHz bandwidth and 3~dBi per element gain, while full module level measurements show 54~dBm saturated EIRP, a near ideal 35~dB gain increase from 64 element combining, and beam steering exceeding $\pm40^{\circ}$ without pattern tapering figures that illustrate the multiplicative benefit of coherent array combining once package level integration removes feed line loss \cite{ref54}. A related wideband organic AiP supporting the n257/n258/n261 bands (24.25-29.5~GHz) integrates an $8\times8$ dual polarized array with 5.1~mm element spacing using a magnetoelectric dipole radiator, achieving over 6~GHz bandwidth in a compact $42.5\times42.5\times1.65$~mm form factor suitable for handset integration. At the single element level, a differential fed, dual aperture, quasi end fire 5G mmWave AiP design covering the n257 band (26.5-29.5~GHz) achieves a peak sub-array gain of 8.2~dBi with $10^{\circ}$--$27^{\circ}$ of additional beam tilt toward end fire, useful for edge mounted handset antennas \cite{ref53}. Thermal management of closely stacked RFIC and antenna layers, dielectric and metallization tolerance across the multilayer stack up, and electromagnetic coupling between adjacent package layers remain the principal AiP design constraints.

