\section{Challenges and Future Research Directions}

\subsection{Propagation Loss}
Millimetre wave and THz signals experience path loss governed by $\lambda^2$ in the Friis equation, compounded by atmospheric absorption windows and non line of sight blockage. Future antenna systems must therefore provide both high boresight gain and fast adaptive beam re steering to maintain link margin as user or blockage geometry changes \cite{ref7}.

\subsection{Hardware Complexity}
Large antenna arrays and RIS surfaces require many RF components, phase control circuits, and bias networks. Reducing RF chain count (via hybrid beamforming or dynamic metasurface antennas) while retaining beamforming gain remains an open system design problem \cite{ref12,ref23}.

\subsection{Energy Efficiency}
Large arrays and high frequency RF chains consume significant DC power. Passive technologies RIS, metasurfaces, and DRAs are attractive precisely because they shift gain from active RF chains to passive electromagnetic structure, but their control circuitry still draws power that must be minimized as element counts grow into the thousands.

\subsection{Reconfigurability and Control Overhead}
Reconfigurable antennas, RIS, and fluid antenna systems all provide adaptive operation, but each introduces a control or estimation overhead bias switching transients, RIS channel estimation complexity that scales with element count, and FAS port scanning delay that must be kept below the coherence time of the channel without materially degrading net throughput \cite{ref19,ref32}.


\subsection{Integrated 6G Antenna Platforms}
Future 6G devices and base stations may combine massive MIMO, metasurfaces/RIS, reconfigurable antennas, sub THz operation, and fluid antenna elements within a single platform. Developing such heterogeneous antenna systems while maintaining low cost, low power, and high reliability and while providing a unified control interface across technologies with very different reconfiguration time-scales is a major open research direction.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper reviewed recent advances in antenna technologies for 6G wireless communication, with a focus on the underlying electromagnetic mechanisms and quantitative, citable performance data. Massive MIMO and millimeter wave antenna arrays provide high spatial capacity and directional transmission through coherent array combining, with reported base station array gains now in the 20-22~dBi range \cite{ref44,ref45}. Reconfigurable antennas provide adaptive frequency, polarization, and radiation characteristics from a single aperture using PIN diode or varactor switching networks. Dielectric resonator antennas provide high radiation efficiency at high frequencies, and antenna in package technology enables compact, low loss integration of antennas with RF circuits, as demonstrated by 54~dBm EIRP from a 64 element 28~GHz AiP module \cite{ref54}.

Reconfigurable intelligent surfaces, dynamic metasurface antennas, holographic MIMO surfaces, and fluid antenna systems represent a newer class of technologies that control the spatial characteristics of the wireless channel itself rather than only the transmitter or receiver aperture. Fabricated RIS prototypes have already demonstrated 15-28~dBi surface gain across sub 6~GHz to mmWave bands \cite{ref20}. Although these technologies offer significant opportunities, challenges related to propagation loss, hardware and control complexity, fabrication tolerance, energy consumption, thermal management, and channel estimation overhead remain unresolved. Future research should focus on AI assisted, highly integrated, wideband, energy efficient, and reconfigurable antenna systems capable of meeting the diverse and simultaneous requirements of 6G wireless networks.

